---
epr-meta-version: 1
id: claude-data-ledgers-governance
covers: subtree
purpose: >
  Deterministic ledgers and cursors owned by hooks, sentinels, and harvesters —
  machine-written, append-mostly (ci-findings.jsonl, deprecations.jsonl,
  ci-cursor.json, dev-intent.jsonl, runtime-findings.jsonl and kin). NOT a
  document store: prose analyses, RCAs, handoffs, and readiness notes that are
  born here never get reconciled into canon (the context-leak-runaway class,
  operator-flagged 2026-07-02 — eleven loose conductor-leak-*.md siblings are
  the scar tissue).
rules:
  - id: route-prose-to-canon
    class: dispatch
    when: { write: "*.md", new: true }
    route-to: { dest: genesis/data/timeline/backlog/ }
    parameters:
      dispatch-agent: librarian
      dispatch-prompt: >
        Review the proposed new markdown artifact for context-pollution. Return exactly one
        recommendation—KEEP HERE, ROUTE with a canonical destination, MERGE into an existing
        artifact, or DROP as conversation-scoped—with the shortest supporting reason. Do not
        create, move, or rewrite files; report to the editing agent and let development continue.
    why: >
      .claude/data is for machine-owned ledgers (*.jsonl, *.json), not prose.
      A concern/RCA/analysis doc belongs in genesis/data/timeline/backlog/
      (timeline-CONVENTIONS-conformant, status-carrying); a design belongs in
      genesis/docs/superpowers/specs|plans/ (born with cites:); a resolved
      investigation graduates to genesis/docs/content/elohim-protocol/history/;
      a session handoff's durable facts belong in memory files or as commentary
      citing a habit (genesis/manifests/habits.yaml). If none of those fit,
      that is usually the signal the content is conversation-scoped — let it go.
  - id: new-ledger-declares-mode-reader-and-drain
    class: inject
    when: { write: "*.jsonl", new: true }
    dedupe-of: ".claude/scripts/_lib/findings_ledger.py (the shared row primitive and the live-set vs append-only mode contract)"
    why: >
      A new ledger here IS a legitimate birth — this directory's own guide says so — and that is
      exactly where the cost is decided, so decide it before the first row.
      (1) REACH the row primitive: _lib/findings_ledger.py already owns the shape ({fp, first_seen,
      status, ...}), the flock'd read-check-append, and the drain. A second hand-rolled appender is
      a second ontology.
      (2) DECLARE THE MODE, because the two are not interchangeable: LIVE-SET (one row per live
      condition, DELETED when it drains — reading answers "what is wrong now") or APPEND-ONLY
      (every row a historical fact, never removed — reading answers "what happened"). An
      append-only ledger that gets drained destroys the growth-rate measure it exists to compute.
      (3) NAME THE READER, by path, in this manifest. governance-findings.jsonl reached 7,806,004
      bytes with zero readers — a repo-wide grep for read/load/open against its name returns
      nothing — and genesis/manifests/habits.yaml has said so since it was 4.3MB. Two more ledgers
      here are 0 bytes. A writer without a reader is the instrument-with-no-reader shape this layer
      has grown at least six times.
      (4) DECLARE A FORGETTING RULE (a count, an age, or a comet tier), and if any row can be
      standing evidence for an open question, a PIN that survives it — SET AND RELEASED BY A
      DECISION, NEVER BY A CLOCK. Rotation without a pin deletes the evidence for an unresolved
      question on a counter; a pin without a release is a leak wearing a policy's clothes. The
      shape already exists four times in this repo and is nowhere named: Jenkins
      buildDiscarder(logRotator(numToKeepStr)) runs at 20/100/50/30/30 with no keepLog pin
      anywhere; memkit-retention.py's HEAD/TAIL/CORE comet keeps a permanent spine but releases it
      automatically; .epr-meta `retire-when:` is a decision-gated pin for RULES; habits.yaml
      `best_observed:` is one for a high-water mark. Nothing pins a result or a ledger row.
    retire-when: >
      when a ledger under .claude/data cannot be opened except through findings_ledger.py, so mode,
      reader, forgetting rule and pin are structural rather than remembered
---

# .claude/data — deterministic ledgers, not a document store

Machine-written accumulator files (JSONL ledgers, JSON cursors, locks) that the
flag→agent→canon→stasis automation reads and writes. New ledger files for new
sentinels are legitimate births. Prose `.md` files are not: they are the
memory-dump drift wearing a data-directory costume, and every one that lands
here bypasses the reconciliation rails (decompose, cites, timeline status).
The background librarian review classifies them while the context still exists,
without stopping the active development pass.
